latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Shapes:\n   - Scan the grid to identify distinct shapes based on color.\n   - For each shape, store its color and list of coordinates.\n\n2. Analyze Shapes:\n   - For each shape, calculate:\n     a. Its vertical bounds (top and bottom rows).\n     b. Its "weight distribution" (ratio of pixels in the lower half vs. upper half).\n     c. Its contribution to the overall center of mass of the image.\n\n3. Calculate Initial Image Metrics:\n   - Compute the overall center of mass of the image.\n   - Calculate a balance score based on how centered the center of mass is.\n   - Compute a vertical symmetry score for the entire image.\n\n4. Generate Flip Combinations:\n   - Create a list of all possible combinations of flipped and non-flipped shapes.\n   - This will be 2^n combinations, where n is the number of shapes, so we may need to limit this for larger numbers of shapes.\n\n5. Evaluate Flip Combinations:\n   - For each combination:\n     a. Create a new grid with the specified shapes flipped.\n     b. Calculate the new center of mass, balance score, and symmetry score.\n     c. Calculate a "weight distribution score" based on how many shapes have more pixels in their lower half.\n     d. Combine these scores into an overall "quality" score for this combination.\n\n6. Select Best Combination:\n   - Choose the combination with the highest overall quality score.\n\n7. Apply Best Combination:\n   - Create the final grid by applying the flips from the best combination.\n\n8. Reconstruct and Return Grid:\n   - Convert the final arrangement back into a ColoredGrid object.\n   - Return this as the solution.\n\nImplementation Notes:\n- We\'ll need to carefully balance the different factors in our quality score. The exact weights might need tuning based on further analysis of test cases.\n- For efficiency, we might want to implement early stopping in our combination evaluation if we find a combination that\'s significantly better than the original.\n- If the number of shapes is large, we might need to use a heuristic approach (like a genetic algorithm) instead of checking all combinations.\n- We should ensure that our flipping operation always keeps shapes within their original vertical bounds.\n\nThis plan addresses the key insights from our reflection:\n- It considers both local (shape-specific) and global (whole image) criteria.\n- It allows for some shapes to be flipped while others remain unchanged.\n- It aims for overall balance and improved weight distribution, not just symmetry.\n- It preserves the horizontal position of shapes.\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to create a solution that handles all the observed patterns in the test cases and generalizes well to new cases.'
